INT. JURY ROOM, FLOODED COURTHOUSE ANNEX - DAY

Ankle-deep water on the floor. Sandbags at the door. A long table, six chairs. Rain drums on a high window.

Five jurors sit with their feet on rungs. Nobody has taken a coat off.

BEATRICE ACHTERBERG, 60s, foreperson, folds her hands. DEMETRIUS, 40s, a bus driver, taps a pen. LOLA PINEDA, 20s, a barista, chews her thumbnail. SAMUEL ODHIAMBO, 50s, a retired teacher, stares at the ceiling. KRZYSZTOF, 30s, a welder, sits with arms crossed.

The verdict form lies in the middle of the table.

BEATRICE
We should vote.

Nobody moves.

BEATRICE (CONT'D)
Anyone?

DEMETRIUS
Not guilty.

LOLA
Not guilty.

SAMUEL
(after a moment)
Not guilty.

KRZYSZTOF
Guilty.

Beatrice nods. Makes a mark. Her pencil is steady.

BEATRICE
Three to one. And I've not voted.

KRZYSZTOF
Vote.

BEATRICE
After we talk.

DEMETRIUS
What's there to talk about?

KRZYSZTOF
The knife.

DEMETRIUS
The knife wasn't his.

KRZYSZTOF
The glove was.

LOLA
(quietly)
Gloves can be borrowed.

KRZYSZTOF
So can knives.

Samuel lowers his gaze from the ceiling. He picks a pencil off the floor, wipes it on his sleeve.

SAMUEL
I taught his mother.

BEATRICE
Mr. Odhiambo.

SAMUEL
I'm not saying it should matter.

BEATRICE
We were told to disregard everything but the evidence.

SAMUEL
I know. I know that.

Silence. The window rattles. A little water laps against the table leg.

LOLA
My coffee machine's underwater.

DEMETRIUS
Mine's my bus.

KRZYSZTOF
Mine's a pay stub.

Nobody laughs. Beatrice smooths the form with both palms.

BEATRICE
Then we take another look at the knife.

The door opens. Water sloshes across the floor.

BAILIFF GWENDOLYN, 40s, in rubber boots and a yellow slicker, wades in with a plastic crate.

GWENDOLYN
Lunch. Egg sandwiches.

She sets the crate on the table. Hands out wrapped sandwiches. Skips Krzysztof. Gives him a longer look instead.

KRZYSZTOF
What?

GWENDOLYN
Nothing.

KRZYSZTOF
You looked at me.

GWENDOLYN
I look at everyone.

She leaves. The door clunks shut.

Beatrice unwraps a sandwich. Doesn't eat. Lola eats half, wraps the rest. Demetrius takes one bite and puts it down. Samuel holds his without opening it. Krzysztof leaves his on the table.

BEATRICE
I'm going to vote.

DEMETRIUS
Go ahead.

BEATRICE
Not guilty.

LOLA
Four.

KRZYSZTOF
(low)
Five hundred and fifty dollars a week.

BEATRICE
Sorry?

KRZYSZTOF
That's what I make. That's what he stole from me.

SAMUEL
You knew the defendant?

KRZYSZTOF
(ignoring this)
I want to see the knife again.

BEATRICE
That requires a note to the judge.

KRZYSZTOF
Write the note.

Beatrice looks at the others. Lola nods. Demetrius shrugs. Samuel sighs and hands her a sheet of paper.

She writes. Folds. Passes it to Samuel, who pushes it back along the table to Krzysztof. He doesn't touch it.

KRZYSZTOF (CONT'D)
You write it.

BEATRICE
I did.

KRZYSZTOF
Then I'll carry it.

He takes the note. Stands. Water swirls around his boots. He wades to the door, and Lola's eyes follow him, wide.

LOLA
(whispering, after he's gone)
Did he say he knew him?

DEMETRIUS
He said it.

SAMUEL
Then he should have said so at the start.

BEATRICE
He didn't want to be excused.

Nobody speaks. Rain on the window. The water, still rising.
